{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>A sorceress you shall not let live.</b> The nature of sorcery is the joining of things disparate one from another; and when one joins those things below, one with another, so too do the forces above join and intermingle, one with another, and there emerges from among them a wondrous, manifest effect. And the prohibition of diverse kinds (kil'im) inclines somewhat toward this, for it mixes the forces above among which separation and distance are proper, and whose joining is extremely harmful." "<b>And behold,</b> the essence of the wisdom of the magicians of Egypt was the matter of sorcery, and by means of it they would perform wondrous things in a natural manner. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: why is it called כשפים? Because they contradict the heavenly retinue (פמליא של מעלה). And the contradiction of the retinue is when a person effects below a combination of things that are the opposite of the simple forces decreed by the motion of the spheres and by the decree of the 'agents' (עירין), which are souls to them, and which are called the retinue on high. And it is proper for a person to leave the world to proceed in its course and according to its simple nature, which is the will of his Creator, blessed be He, who made it thus; and if one engages in sorcery, there is in this an aspect of contradiction. And such is the view of the Ramban." "<b>But</b> the view of our Master Hananel of blessed memory in his commentary in tractate Sanhedrin — 'they contradict' means they appear as though they contradict — and he said that sorceries have no effect except what the Holy One, blessed be He, decrees, in the manner of what R. Hanina said to that woman: ״אין עוד מלבדו״ is written. And even though they answer there, 'R. Hanina is different, for his merit is abundant,' that answer is a mere deflection and we do not rely upon it; and the refutation of the challenge to R. Hanina stands.'" "<b>And if</b> you should ask: since sorceries have no effect except in a place where the Holy One, blessed be He, decrees, why did the Torah forbid them, and why did it impose the death penalty upon the sorcerer? And the reason is because he transgressed the decree of the Holy One, blessed be He, to do what He withheld from him. Now, if one who transgresses the decree of a king of flesh and blood is liable to death for his decree, then for the decree of the King of the king of kings, the Holy One, blessed be He, all the more so. Thus far his words of blessed memory. And because Israel were accustomed to this wisdom, for they were now newly come forth from Egypt, and the matter was very close that people might go astray after it, therefore Scripture needed to prevent them by forbidding this wisdom. And in order to add stringency in the matter, Scripture did not say 'a sorceress — you shall surely put to death' (מות תמות), but rather 'you shall not let live' (לא תחיה), for Scripture wished to intensify the matter with a negative commandment not to let her live, just as it intensified in the case of the seven nations and said (Deuteronomy 20:16): ״לא תחיה כל נשמה״. And it mentioned 'a sorceress you shall not let live' and not 'a sorcerer,' because sorcery is more common among women, and the practice of the 'ov is performed only by a woman. Therefore Scripture said (Leviticus 20:27): ״ואיש או אשה כי יהיה בהם אוב או ידעוני״. And they already mentioned in the books of sorceries that the matter of the 'ov is: a woman standing at the grave of the deceased at its head, and the man at its feet, and a youth in the middle with a rattle (קשקוש) in his hand, clanging and rattling with it. And behold, this became customary among the nations in those times exceedingly; from there it was transferred to their places, and this belongs to no other nation but emerged for them from this root, which is 'a root that bears gall and wormwood' (ראש ולענה)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:03:06.239333632Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}